Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• FORUMMESSAGEVOTES."FromUserId": user who cast the up‑vote – needed to count votes given.  
• FORUMMESSAGEVOTES."ToUserId": user who received the up‑vote – needed to count votes received.  
• FORUMMESSAGEVOTES."ForumMessageId": lets us count DISTINCT forum‑messages in case the same user voted more than once for the same recipient’s message.  
• USERS."Id": primary key to link user ids to their names.  
• USERS."UserName": Kaggle handle to be shown in the final output (optionally also USERS."DisplayName").

Typical conditions we will use  
• GROUP BY "FromUserId","ToUserId" to aggregate vote counts.  
• COUNT(DISTINCT "ForumMessageId") to get the number of unique up‑votes.  
• A self‑join (or two separate aggregates) to look at the reverse direction ("To" → "From").  
• Ordering by the received‑count DESC, then the given‑back count DESC.  
• LIMIT 1 (or 20 while exploring) to keep result sets small.

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that gradually build toward the final answer.  
All identifiers are wrapped in double quotes and fully qualified with DATABASE.SCHEMA.TABLE.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Quick peek at raw forum‑message votes
SELECT *
FROM "META_KAGGLE"."META_KAGGLE"."FORUMMESSAGEVOTES"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Inspect how many votes any single giver → receiver pair has (simple COUNT)
SELECT 
       "FromUserId",
       "ToUserId",
       COUNT(*)                  AS "total_votes"
FROM   "META_KAGGLE"."META_KAGGLE"."FORUMMESSAGEVOTES"
GROUP  BY "FromUserId","ToUserId"
ORDER  BY "total_votes" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Same as #2 but counting DISTINCT "ForumMessageId" for robustness
SELECT 
       "FromUserId",
       "ToUserId",
       COUNT(DISTINCT "ForumMessageId") AS "distinct_votes"
FROM   "META_KAGGLE"."META_KAGGLE"."FORUMMESSAGEVOTES"
GROUP  BY "FromUserId","ToUserId"
ORDER  BY "distinct_votes" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Pull the top 20 giver → receiver pairs together with the reverse direction counts
WITH pair_counts AS (
    SELECT 
           "FromUserId" AS "giver",
           "ToUserId"   AS "receiver",
           COUNT(DISTINCT "ForumMessageId") AS "votes_forward"
    FROM   "META_KAGGLE"."META_KAGGLE"."FORUMMESSAGEVOTES"
    GROUP  BY "FromUserId","ToUserId"
)
SELECT 
       pc1."giver",
       pc1."receiver",
       pc1."votes_forward",
       COALESCE(pc2."votes_forward",0) AS "votes_back"
FROM   pair_counts pc1
LEFT   JOIN pair_counts pc2
       ON pc1."giver"    = pc2."receiver"
      AND pc1."receiver" = pc2."giver"
ORDER  BY pc1."votes_forward" DESC, "votes_back" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Bring usernames into the picture for the 20 strongest one‑way relationships
WITH pair_counts AS (
    SELECT 
           "FromUserId" AS "giver",
           "ToUserId"   AS "receiver",
           COUNT(DISTINCT "ForumMessageId") AS "votes_forward"
    FROM   "META_KAGGLE"."META_KAGGLE"."FORUMMESSAGEVOTES"
    GROUP  BY "FromUserId","ToUserId"
),
paired AS (
    SELECT 
           pc1.*,
           COALESCE(pc2."votes_forward",0) AS "votes_back"
    FROM   pair_counts pc1
    LEFT   JOIN pair_counts pc2
           ON pc1."giver"    = pc2."receiver"
          AND pc1."receiver" = pc2."giver"
)
SELECT 
       ug."UserName"  AS "giver_username",
       ur."UserName"  AS "receiver_username",
       p."votes_forward" AS "votes_given",
       p."votes_back"    AS "votes_returned"
FROM   paired p
JOIN   "META_KAGGLE"."META_KAGGLE"."USERS" ug
       ON p."giver" = ug."Id"
JOIN   "META_KAGGLE"."META_KAGGLE"."USERS" ur
       ON p."receiver" = ur."Id"
ORDER  BY p."votes_given" DESC, p."votes_back" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Show only pairs where votes_given > votes_returned to focus on asymmetry
WITH pair_counts AS (
    SELECT 
           "FromUserId" AS "giver",
           "ToUserId"   AS "receiver",
           COUNT(DISTINCT "ForumMessageId") AS "votes_forward"
    FROM   "META_KAGGLE"."META_KAGGLE"."FORUMMESSAGEVOTES"
    GROUP  BY "FromUserId","ToUserId"
),
paired AS (
    SELECT 
           pc1.*,
           COALESCE(pc2."votes_forward",0) AS "votes_back"
    FROM   pair_counts pc1
    LEFT   JOIN pair_counts pc2
           ON pc1."giver"    = pc2."receiver"
          AND pc1."receiver" = pc2."giver"
)
SELECT 
       ug."UserName"  AS "giver_username",
       ur."UserName"  AS "receiver_username",
       p."votes_forward" AS "votes_given",
       p."votes_back"    AS "votes_returned"
FROM   paired p
JOIN   "META_KAGGLE"."META_KAGGLE"."USERS" ug
       ON p."giver" = ug."Id"
JOIN   "META_KAGGLE"."META_KAGGLE"."USERS" ur
       ON p."receiver" = ur."Id"
WHERE  p."votes_forward" > p."votes_back"
ORDER  BY p."votes_forward" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
